    "body": "Child of #416 (decomposition approved by the maintainer on 2026-11-27; key `plan-floor`, after: `save-output`). Decomposition: https://github.com/mintswarm/mintswarm/issues/416/artifacts/3\n\n## Problem\n\nplan-approval/floor.py embeds third-party text in its reasons:\n- the footprint \"not valid JSON: {exc}\" (967);\n- the config \"does not convert to JSON: {exc}\" (706);\n- shlex's \"command does not split: {exc}\" (819);\n- OSError text at 530, 605, 617, 688, 699, 875, 1180, 1256 and 1530;\n- its crash handler prints str(exc) (1615).\nThe Rust side has the same kinds of problem:\n- rules::json's ReadErrorKind has kinds that Python cannot tell apart without reading\n  exc.msg;\n- crates/config/src/schema.rs:1268-1269 copies Python's shlex wording\n  (\"No closing quotation\", \"No escaped character\");\n- the Rust plan floor gets its input from the runner's\n  crates/runner/src/exec/git/bundle.rs. The only io::ErrorKind-to-phrase mapping lives\n  in crates/runner/src/tool/save_output.rs.\nThe parity data and stubs need attention too:\n- crates/rules/tests/plan_floor.rs compares rule ids and results only, not reasons;\n- crates/rules/tests/fixtures/plan_floor.jsonl holds Python's current reasons;\n- crates/config/tests/conformance/cases.json (about lines 518-546) stubs yq's error\n  text.\n\n## Suggested approach\n\n- floor.py's existing classes (InputError, FootprintError, DocumentError, ScreenError,\n  RecordError) derive from the shared base. Every place a failure occurs follows\n  parse_screen's pattern: a fixed cause, raised `from exc`.\n- JSON messages follow q3 (a):\n  - only the kinds we detect ourselves keep their own message: duplicate key,\n    disallowed constant, nesting depth;\n  - everything else is \"not valid JSON\" with a computed line;\n  - rules::json collapses the kinds Python cannot tell apart, to match.\n- shlex failures use the port's wording (\"a quote is not closed\", \"a backslash ends\n  the command\"), and crates/config's schema.rs takes the same wording (q7 (a)). Python\n  tells the two cases apart by inspecting the command itself, not the exc text.\n- The io::ErrorKind phrase mapping moves from crates/runner/src/tool/save_output.rs\n  into crates/rules as a pure function, so there is one definition.\n  - The runner's save-output tool and its plan-floor bundle errors (BundleError) both\n    use it.\n  - floor.py uses the matching phrases from the shared Python module.\n- Refresh plan_floor.jsonl with plan_floor_capture.py, and widen plan_floor.rs to\n  compare reasons.\n- Sweep the yq error-text stub in cases.json. If it is only stub input that no\n  assertion compares against, it stays. Any test that compares output against it is\n  changed to check the error class.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- No change to any rule's result, exit class or the floor record beyond reason wording.\n- decision.py, size.py and the decomposition floor belong to plan-decision.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- No reason or other output line of plan-approval/floor.py contains text from a JSON decoder, YAML loader, shlex, the OS or git.\n- floor.py's crash handler prints a fixed line naming the exception class and exits 2.\n- rules::plan_floor and floor.py print the same reason for each footprint, config, shlex and OS failure case in plan-approval/selftest.py.\n- crates/config/src/schema.rs no longer contains \"No closing quotation\" or \"No escaped character\"; it uses the shlex port's wording.\n- The io::ErrorKind-to-phrase mapping is defined once, in crates/rules; crates/runner/src/tool/save_output.rs and the runner's plan-floor bundle errors use it, and the save-output parity tests still pass.\n- crates/rules/tests/plan_floor.rs compares each rule's reason as well as its id and result, against plan_floor.jsonl regenerated by plan_floor_capture.py, and passes on every Python 3 version CI runs.\n- No test in crates/config compares any output against the yq error text stubbed in crates/config/tests/conformance/cases.json.\n- No test in plan-approval/selftest.py, crates/rules, crates/config or crates/runner asserts on third-party error wording.\n",
